  "prompt": "A high-speed train accelerates uniformly along a straight track from 54 km h^-1 to 90 km h^-1 in a time of 25 s. What is the acceleration of the train in m s^-2?",
  "options": [
    "0.40 m s^-2",
    "1.44 m s^-2",
    "2.16 m s^-2",
    "3.60 m s^-2"
  ],
  "answer": {
    "correct_option": 1,
    "explanation": "Convert velocities to m s^-1: 54 km h^-1 = 54 / 3.6 = 15 m s^-1; 90 km h^-1 = 90 / 3.6 = 25 m s^-1. Then a = (25 - 15) / 25 = 10 / 25 = 0.40 m s^-2."
  },
  "distractor_explanations": [
    "Correct: 54 km h^-1 = 15 m s^-1, 90 km h^-1 = 25 m s^-1, a = (25 - 15) / 25 = 0.40 m s^-2.",
    "Calculates (90 - 54) / 25 = 1.44, failing to convert km h^-1 to m s^-1.",
    "Applies an incorrect conversion multiplier.",
    "Divides 90 by 25 without converting units or subtracting initial speed."
  ],
  "hints": [
    "Convert both speeds from km h^-1 to m s^-1 by dividing by 3.6.",
    "Use a = (v - u) / t with SI units."
  ],
  "solution": "A. u = 15 m s^-1, v = 25 m s^-1. a = (25 - 15) / 25 = 0.40 m s^-2.",
  "teacher_walkthrough": [
    "Demonstrate unit conversion: 1 km h^-1 = 1000 m / 3600 s = 1 / 3.6 m s^-1.",
    "u = 54 / 3.6 = 15 m s^-1 and v = 90 / 3.6 = 25 m s^-1.",
    "a = (25 - 15) / 25 = 10 / 25 = 0.40 m s^-2."
